WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:02.908
Welcome to SysNDD.

00:03.042 --> 00:08.226
Here is how your code and your AI
assistant reach its curated data.

00:08.567 --> 00:14.383
How do you get SysNDD data into your scripts,
or into an AI assistant?

00:14.516 --> 00:18.390
In four parts: the API, one gene, the evidence,

00:18.524 --> 00:22.676
and MCP, the Model Context Protocol.

00:23.033 --> 00:26.252
Your own scripts can read SysNDD through its

00:26.386 --> 00:30.383
public application programming interface, or API.

00:30.517 --> 00:35.095
The OpenAPI logo in the page
footer opens this Swagger page.

00:35.229 --> 00:39.514
It lists all endpoints and the API version.

00:39.867 --> 00:43.788
Reading needs no account, no key and no token.

00:43.921 --> 00:48.359
Public data sits in groups
such as entity, gene and panels.

00:48.492 --> 00:54.987
Only endpoints that submit, review,
curate or administer data need a login.

00:55.333 --> 00:58.756
Our example gene is <i>ANKRD11</i>.

00:58.889 --> 01:01.452
In the entity group, open the entity list.

01:01.585 --> 01:03.276
Select Try it out.

01:03.409 --> 01:08.105
In the filter field, type equals, symbol, <i>ANKRD11</i>.

01:08.238 --> 01:10.455
Then select Execute.

01:10.800 --> 01:14.366
The answer comes back as JSON, a text format.

01:14.499 --> 01:21.499
It holds one entity, number 45:
<i>ANKRD11</i> with KBG syndrome, autosomal dominant,

01:22.273 --> 01:25.280
evidence category Definitive.

01:25.633 --> 01:31.721
Above the answer, Swagger shows
the matching curl command and the request URL.

01:31.855 --> 01:34.290
Copy either into your own script.

01:34.424 --> 01:39.474
The gene and panels lists use the same filter syntax.

01:39.833 --> 01:43.691
Five endpoints return the evidence for an entity:

01:43.825 --> 01:46.633
the review with its clinical synopsis,

01:46.767 --> 01:53.512
the status with its category,
phenotypes, variation terms and publications.

01:53.867 --> 01:57.275
The website shows the same approved record.

01:57.408 --> 02:00.181
On the entity page for number 45,

02:00.314 --> 02:05.625
the synopsis and the publication list match the API.

02:05.967 --> 02:10.806
For AI assistants, SysNDD runs an MCP server.

02:10.939 --> 02:13.806
In the menu, open Help, then MCP.

02:13.939 --> 02:17.756
The server is read-only: it cannot change data.

02:18.100 --> 02:21.026
Point your assistant at the server address.

02:21.159 --> 02:26.099
Leave authentication unset; no account is needed.

02:26.467 --> 02:31.752
For Claude Code,
one command in a terminal adds the server.

02:32.100 --> 02:38.704
Now ask, for example:
which SysNDD entities exist for <i>ANKRD11</i>?

02:38.837 --> 02:44.778
The assistant calls a SysNDD
tool and receives the same entity 45.

02:45.133 --> 02:49.010
An assistant can misread or overstate what it retrieves,

02:49.144 --> 02:51.912
so check the evidence on the entity page.

02:52.046 --> 02:55.407
Some tools also return NDDScore.

02:55.541 --> 03:02.331
It helps prioritize genes,
but it is a prediction, not an evidence category.

03:02.700 --> 03:06.811
Remember: reading needs no account, key or token.

03:06.945 --> 03:12.170
One filter finds a gene's entities;
the entity ID opens the evidence.

03:12.303 --> 03:16.782
Check the record before you rely on an AI answer.

03:17.133 --> 03:22.221
Find the Swagger page and
the MCP page on the SysNDD website.

03:22.354 --> 03:27.694
The documentation chapter
on the API and MCP has more examples.
